HPA requires Metrics Server running so it can scrape CPU and memory usage from the Kubelet.

1. Enable Metrics Server

minikube addons enable metrics-server

Verify metrics are being collected (wait 30–60s for the first scrape):

kubectl top nodes
kubectl top pods -n mini-project-1

(Once you see CPU/Memory readouts rather than errors, the metrics pipeline is operational).

2. Create the HPA Manifest (05-hpa.yaml)T

This scales the frontend deployment between 2 and 6 replicas when CPU usage exceeds 50% of the pod's CPU 
request (requests: cpu: 50m --> 25m threshold).

apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: frontend-hpa
  namespace: mini-project-1
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: frontend-deployment
  minReplicas: 2
  maxReplicas: 6
  metrics:
    - type: Resource
      resource:
        name: cpu
        target:
          type: Utilization
          averageUtilization: 50

Apply it:

kubectl apply -f 05-hpa.yaml

kubectl get hpa -n mini-project-1 -w

3. Run the Load Generator

In a separate terminal, launch a lightweight container that runs a tight loop sending requests to the frontend service:
Run a temporary ApacheBench (ab) or BusyBox pod to flood the frontend service:

kubectl run -i --tty load-generator --rm --image=busybox:1.28 --restart=Never -n mini-project-1 -- /bin/sh -c "while true; do wget -q -O- http://frontend-svc; done"

Verification: Under TARGETS, you will initially see <unknown>/50% which will transition to 0%/50% or similar within a minute.

In another terminal, watch the HPA and pod count surge:

kubectl get hpa -n mini-project-1 -w
kubectl get pods -n mini-project-1 -l app=frontend -w

You will see replicas increase from 2 --> 4 --> 6. When you stop the load generator (Ctrl+C), HPA stabilizes 
and scales back down after the cooldown period (default 5 minutes).


